WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:07.049
There's a super interesting new piece in the
nation written by Sasha Abramski, which says

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:12.560
that there are now even some Republicans worried
that indeed Donald Trump is trying to figure

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.400
out how to retain control regardless of the
outcome of the November election, which is

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.340
now under eight weeks away.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:24.130
It's eight weeks minus one day.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:31.210
And they're actually using the word coup to
describe the possibility of what Donald Trump

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.160
may do if indeed he loses to Joe Biden on
November 3rd.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.930
And this article by Sasha Abramski in The
Nation, which we're linking to and I encourage

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.260
you to read quotes Charles Freed.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.570
Charles Freed is a Harvard Law School professor,
still a Republican, as far as far as I know,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:46.730
right now.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.809
And Charles Freed is increasingly worried
about Donald Trump's electoral strategy of

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:57.170
seemingly encouraging chaos and violence and
is even talking about how this strategy is

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:03.969
ripe for Donald Trump to actually send in
provocateurs into largely left leaning protests

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:10.390
in order to make them seem more violent, in
order to try to justify his reelection or

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:17.040
as a sort of double goal to claim that things
are so crazy we can't really be sure the election

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.220
was done correctly.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:23.950
If he doesn't win and this is a classic authoritarian
provocateur strategy, we've seen this the

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:24.950
world over.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.350
We've seen it for decades and decades.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.770
That's Charles Freed.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.220
Michael Steele, the former chair of the Republican
National Committee.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.159
We've talked about him on the program, relatively
well-known individual.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:41.060
He is also concerned about the same possibility
in November.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:46.450
And the difference would be Michael Steele
doesn't so much think that what's going on

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.229
